---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import TopPick from "../components/TopPick.astro";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "How to Waterproof a Basement",
    "url": "/how-to-waterproof-a-basement/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Basement Waterproofing Systems",
    "url": "/best-basement-waterproofing-systems/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Cinder Block Sealers",
    "url": "/best-cinder-block-sealer-reviews/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-18", modified: "2026-11-18" });
const title = "Flex Seal Review (2026): A Good Patch, Not a Permanent Fix";
const description =
  "Flex Seal is a good temporary patch: a 14 oz can covers about 12 sq ft per the maker, but it will not fix a basement wall, a worn roof or a pressurized pipe.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Flex Seal Review",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/flex-seal-review/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Flex Seal any good?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, for what it is designed to do. Owner reviews are mostly positive for small, non-pressurized repairs such as gutter seams, flashing gaps, trailer roofs and planters, provided the surface was clean and dry and several coats went on. Reviews turn negative when people expect it to fix a failing roof, a seeping basement wall or a pressurized pipe permanently."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are the most common Flex Seal complaints?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Owners most often complain that a single spray coat still leaks, that patches peel off damp or dirty surfaces, that the solvent smell is strong, that cans clog before they are empty, and that it failed as a permanent roof or plumbing repair. Most of these trace back to prep, coat thickness or using it beyond what the maker supports."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you use Flex Seal on a basement wall?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On small spots, yes. Flex Seal Liquid can seal around pipe penetrations, window well seams and the outside of a foundation crack. As a whole-wall waterproofer it is a poor choice: it has no published pressure rating, water from the soil side tends to push it off, and a masonry waterproofer covers the same wall for far less money."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you paint over Flex Seal?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on the product. The maker says the spray can be painted any color once it is dry. For Flex Seal Liquid, the maker advises against painting over it. Flex Tape mostly will not take paint, though the maker suggests a coat of the spray as a primer if you want to try."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Flex Seal safe for drinking water?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. The maker says the spray is not for surfaces that contact drinking water, and Flex Tape has not been checked for drinking-water contact. Flex Seal Liquid's page says it is safe for rain barrels once fully cured but does not address potable water. Do not use any of them on water tanks, wells or supply pipes that carry drinking water."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are UAL Flex sealant reviews about Flex Seal?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not necessarily. We found no product sold under the exact name UAL Flex, and several unrelated sealants use Flex in their names, including automotive seam sealers and construction silicones. Check the maker on the label before reading reviews. Flex Seal itself is sold by the Flex Seal Family of Products through flexsealproducts.com and major retailers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does Flex Seal take to dry?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Per the maker, the spray is dry to the touch in about 2 to 3 hours and fully cured in 24 to 48 hours. Flex Seal Liquid is dry to the touch in 6 to 12 hours and cures in 24 to 48 hours. Wait 24 to 48 hours between coats for both, and longer in cool or humid weather."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Basement & Waterproofing",
    tags: [
      "flex seal review",
      "flex seal reviews",
      "flex seal reviews and complaints",
      "what problems does flex seal fix",
      "ual flex sealant reviews",
      "how long does flex seal last",
      "flex seal vs silicone caulk"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Basement Wall Sealer Picks",
    link: "/best-basement-wall-sealer/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <header data-hero class="bg-[#F4F6F8] border-b border-gray-200">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-10 pb-8 lg:pt-14 lg:pb-10">
        <p class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest m-0 mb-4">
          <span class="text-[#D93A00]">Product Review</span>
          <span class="text-gray-300" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
          <span class="text-gray-500">Updated {dates.display}</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-[2.1rem] leading-[1.15] sm:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-gray-900 m-0">
          Flex Seal Review
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg lg:text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-5 mb-0">
          Flex Seal is worth keeping on the shelf for small leaks, gutter seams and emergency patches. It is not a replacement for basement waterproofing, a roof repair or a plumber.
        </p>
        <!-- Author & Meta -->
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mt-7">
          <AuthorAvatar size={40} />
          <div class="min-w-0">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 m-0">Written by <a href="/authors/alex-rivers/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Alex Rivers</a></p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 m-0">Home Improvement Editor &middot; Updated {dates.display}</p>
          </div>
          <SocialProof users="" />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-6">
          <TrustBadge />
          <span class="flex-1 h-px bg-gray-300"></span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3 mb-0">
          Based on Flex Seal's product pages and labels (checked Nov 18, 2026) and aggregated owner reviews. Not hands-on tested. No paid placements. <a href="/how-we-test/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 underline">Read how we pick</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <details open class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl px-5 pt-4 pb-2">
        <summary class="flex items-center gap-3 pb-3 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" d="M9 6h11M9 12h11M9 18h11M4.5 6h.01M4.5 12h.01M4.5 18h.01" /></svg>
          <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">In This Guide</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-gray-400">13 sections</span>
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 ml-auto text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M6 15l6-6 6 6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6">
              <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">1.</span><span>The Short Answer</span></a>
              <a href="#what-it-fixes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">2.</span><span>What Each Product Fixes</span></a>
              <a href="#specs" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">3.</span><span>Maker Specs</span></a>
              <a href="#how-long" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">4.</span><span>How Long It Lasts</span></a>
              <a href="#how-much" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">5.</span><span>How Much You Need</span></a>
              <a href="#complaints" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">6.</span><span>Owner Complaints</span></a>
              <a href="#basement" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">7.</span><span>Basement Walls</span></a>
              <a href="#roofs-gutters-pipes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">8.</span><span>Roofs, Gutters and Pipes</span></a>
              <a href="#vs-caulk" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">9.</span><span>Flex Seal vs Caulk</span></a>
              <a href="#apply" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">10.</span><span>How to Apply It</span></a>
              <a href="#who" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>Who Should Buy It</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">12.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">13.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Flex Seal is a good temporary patch for small leaks: the maker rates a 14 oz spray can at about 12 square feet, enough for a gutter seam or a cracked flashing, not a whole wall. How well it holds depends on prep, coat thickness and sun. This review covers each product in the family, the maker's specs, owner complaints and who should skip it.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Flex Seal Reviews: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Flex Seal does what a rubber coating can do: it bridges small cracks and holes on a clean, dry surface and keeps rain out of them for a while. It cannot hold back water under pressure, rebuild a rotted roof deck or stop a basement wall from wicking groundwater. Owner reviews line up with that split. They are mostly positive for gutter seams, flashing, trailer roofs and quick patches, and mostly negative from people who expected the infomercial result on a big or wet job. The standard 14 oz spray can lists at $14.99 on the maker's store.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Verdict</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Buy it if</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">You need a quick, flexible patch on a gutter seam, a flashing gap, a trailer or shed roof, a planter or an outdoor fixture</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Skip it if</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">You want to waterproof a basement wall, fix a failing roof, or seal a pressurized water, gas or drinking-water line</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Price band</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $15 per 14 oz spray can (maker's store, Nov 18, 2026); Liquid, Paste and Tape cost more per job</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Best member of the family</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flex Seal Liquid for brushable coverage; Flex Tape for an emergency drip; Flex Paste for gaps the spray cannot fill</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Biggest weakness</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thin spray coats and poor adhesion on damp, dirty or oily surfaces</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-fixes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Problems Does Flex Seal Fix?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Flex Seal fixes small, non-pressurized leaks: cracked gutter seams, gaps around vent boots and flashing, pinholes in metal trailer roofs, leaky planters, fountains, bird baths and the joint between two materials that a tube of caulk cannot bridge. The maker's own list of uses is longer, covering roofs, gutters, foundations, pools, RVs, boats and rust protection, but the common thread is the same. Every product in the family is a rubbery material that stays flexible after it cures, so it moves with the surface instead of cracking along the joint.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it is (per the maker)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flex Seal (spray)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Liquid rubber in an aerosol can that dries to a flexible, paintable coating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gutter seams, flashing, small cracks, rust spots, hard-to-reach areas</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flex Seal Liquid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thick, self-leveling liquid rubber you brush, roll, dip or pour</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Larger areas: trailer roofs, planters, the outside face of a foundation crack</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flex Tape</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rubberized tape with a strong adhesive, sold in 4 in rolls and wider sizes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Emergency patches, including on wet surfaces and below the waterline</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flex Paste</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Super-thick rubberized paste applied with a putty knife or trowel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gaps, holes and seams too large for the spray</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flex Shot (now Flex Caulk Silicone)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thick rubber sealant dispensed from a can without a caulk gun</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tubs, sinks, small gaps and weatherizing</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flex Seal spray and Flex Seal Liquid</h3>

          <p>
            The spray is the product most people mean when they say Flex Seal. It comes out as a thick liquid, settles into small cracks and dries to a satin, rubbery skin that the maker says can be painted. Flex Seal Liquid is the same idea in a can you open: a heavier, self-leveling rubber that the maker says you can brush, roll, dip or pour, and which it recommends over the spray whenever you want to brush or roll. Liquid builds a much thicker film per coat, which is why it is the better value for anything bigger than a seam. The maker advises against painting over Liquid.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flex Tape, Flex Paste and Flex Shot</h3>

          <p>
            Flex Tape is the emergency tool. The maker says it bonds to wet or dry surfaces and even under water, which makes it the one to grab for a dripping pipe while you wait for a plumber, though the maker itself calls it a temporary, emergency repair in its pool FAQ. Flex Paste fills the gaps the spray cannot: you trowel it into holes and wide seams, and it skins over in 15 to 30 minutes, per the product page. Flex Shot is a thick rubber sealant in a pressurized can that you dispense like caulk without a gun; the maker's page now says it is sold as Flex Caulk Silicone, with the same formula under a new name.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Are Flex Seal's Specs?
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Spec (source: flexsealproducts.com, checked Nov 18, 2026)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Flex Seal spray</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Flex Seal Liquid</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sizes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 oz mini, 14 oz, 17 oz MAX</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pint, quart, gallon, 2.5 gal MAX</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Maker's coverage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2 / 12 / 15 sq ft per can</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to 18.75 / 37.5 / 150 / 375 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dry to the touch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 to 3 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6 to 12 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Time between coats</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 to 48 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 to 48 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Full cure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 to 48 hours, strengthens over time</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 to 48 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Application temperature</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Surface 0 to 100 F; can above 60 F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">32 to 120 F</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cured temperature range</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">-20 to 140 F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not listed here; see the label</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Paintable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, once dry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not recommended</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Colors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Black, white, clear, gray, plus several colors in 14 oz</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Black, white, clear, gray</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drinking water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not for surfaces that contact drinking water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not addressed; rain barrels OK once cured</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Flex Tape is listed for application from 20 to 120 F and rated to withstand -70 to 200 F once applied, in 4 inch by 5 foot and 4 inch by 15 foot rolls, with 8 and 12 inch widths sold separately. Flex Paste comes in cartridges, half-quart and quart jars and a MAX tub, skins over in 15 to 30 minutes and fully cures in 24 to 48 hours. Flex Shot forms a tacky skin in about 20 minutes and fully cures in 24 to 48 hours. The maker does not publish a service life, a hydrostatic pressure rating or a solids content for any of these products, and that gap is worth noting when you compare them with dedicated coatings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-long" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Long Does Flex Seal Last?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Flex Seal has no published service life, so the honest answer is that it lasts as long as the coat stays bonded and thick enough to stay watertight, which owners report ranges from a single season to several years. The maker's page says many users report it lasting for years without cracking or peeling, and that results depend on the environment, the number of coats and maintenance. That matches the pattern in owner reviews: the long-lived repairs are thick, multi-coat patches on clean metal or masonry, and the short-lived ones are a single misted coat over dirt, rust scale or a damp surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sun is the main enemy of any thin rubber film. A patch on a north-facing gutter seam, out of direct light and shed of standing water, has an easy life. A patch on a flat, south-facing shed roof sits under ultraviolet light and summer heat for hours a day, and owners commonly report that these need a fresh coat after a season or two. Ponding water speeds the decline, because a seam that stays wet for days after rain is under constant attack at its edges.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical way to make it last is to treat it as maintenance rather than a one-time fix. Inspect the patch each spring and fall, when you clean the gutters anyway, and add a coat where you see thinning, pinholes or lifting edges. A Flex Seal repair that you top up every year or two can keep a gutter or a trailer roof dry for a long time; one you forget about will eventually fail, usually at the edges first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-much" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much Flex Seal Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plan on one 14 oz spray can for about 12 square feet of single-coat coverage, per the maker, and plan on two or three coats for anything that has to stay watertight. In practice that means a can per 4 to 6 square feet of finished, multi-coat patch. Flex Seal Liquid goes much further: the maker rates a gallon at up to 150 square feet per coat, so a quart covering up to 37.5 square feet is usually the right size for a handful of seams.
          </p>

          <p>
            Measure the area you will actually coat, not the size of the leak. The maker tells you to spray past the damaged area and feather the edges, and a good patch extends at least a couple of inches beyond the crack on every side. A 6 inch crack in a gutter seam therefore needs a patch closer to 10 by 6 inches, and on a gutter the coating has to wrap the bottom and both sides of the trough to keep water from running around it.
          </p>

