          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="apply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Do You Apply Flex Seal So It Holds?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean it, dry it, then build several thin coats with a full day between them. That is the maker's method, and owner reviews suggest that almost every long-lasting repair followed it. Scrub off dirt, rust scale, loose paint and grime, wipe away oil or grease, and let the surface dry completely. Sanding or lightly etching smooth surfaces improves adhesion, per the maker's Liquid page.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the spray, shake the can well, keep it above 60 F, and spray from 8 to 12 inches away in even, sweeping passes, covering an area wider than the leak and feathering the edges. Let each coat dry and wait 24 to 48 hours before the next. For Liquid, use it straight from the can without thinning, brush or roll it on between 32 and 120 F, and keep it out of the rain for about a day. Clean up uncured Liquid with a dry cloth and mineral spirits, not water.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SAFETY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Ventilate, protect your eyes and keep flames away</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The spray is a flammable aerosol: keep it away from heat, sparks and pilot lights. Work outdoors or with windows open and a fan exhausting air. Wear gloves, eye protection and old clothes; cured rubber is hard to remove. On a ladder, keep three points of contact and do not overreach.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Who Should Buy It, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Flex Seal if you want a flexible, forgiving patch for small, non-pressurized leaks and you are willing to prep properly and apply several coats. It is a sensible thing to keep in the garage for a leaking gutter seam during a storm, a cracked downspout joint, a camper or trailer roof seam, outdoor fountains and planters, rust spots on metal, or the gap around a vent or pipe on the outside of the house. Homeowners who treat it as a stopgap or as part of yearly maintenance tend to be the satisfied reviewers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if your problem is bigger than a patch. A basement wall that seeps needs drainage and a masonry waterproofer, a roof with worn shingles needs a roofer, a supply line under pressure needs a plumber, and anything touching drinking water, gasoline or extreme heat is outside what the maker supports. Skip the spray in particular if you dislike solvent smells and the job is indoors with poor ventilation.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do buy, buy the right member of the family. Pick the spray for seams and hard-to-reach spots, Liquid for anything over a few square feet, Paste for gaps and holes, Tape for a drip that needs stopping right now, and Flex Shot, now sold as Flex Caulk Silicone, for small sealing jobs around tubs and sinks. Matching the product to the job prevents most of the complaints in the reviews.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes With Flex Seal
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying one coat and calling it done</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A single pass from the spray can usually leaves pinholes, especially on rough or porous surfaces. The maker calls for several thin coats with 24 to 48 hours between them, and for more coats until no light shows through. Budget two or three cans for a seam that has to stay watertight.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating a wet or dirty surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flex Seal bonds to clean, dry surfaces. Grime, chalky paint, rust scale and moisture are why so many patches peel. Scrub, degrease and let the area dry fully before you start; if you had to coat a wet surface in an emergency, the maker says to reapply once it is dry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using it to waterproof a whole basement wall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flex Seal Liquid can seal penetrations and small spots, but it carries no hydrostatic pressure rating and costs far more per square foot than a masonry waterproofer. Water pushing from the soil side will blister a surface film. Fix drainage first, then use a coating made for masonry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting it on pressurized or drinking-water lines</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The maker says its products are not made for extreme pressure and the spray is not for surfaces that contact drinking water. Flex Tape can slow a drip for a few hours, but shut off the water and get a proper coupling or a plumber. Never use it on gas lines or fuel tanks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying indoors without ventilation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The spray is a flammable aerosol with a strong solvent smell. Using it in a closed basement or bathroom, especially near a gas appliance pilot light, is unsafe. Open windows, run a fan that exhausts outside, wear eye protection and keep every flame and spark source well away.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Flex Seal any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for what it is designed to do. Owner reviews are mostly positive for small, non-pressurized repairs such as gutter seams, flashing gaps, trailer roofs and planters, provided the surface was clean and dry and several coats went on. Reviews turn negative when people expect it to fix a failing roof, a seeping basement wall or a pressurized pipe permanently.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the most common Flex Seal complaints?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Owners most often complain that a single spray coat still leaks, that patches peel off damp or dirty surfaces, that the solvent smell is strong, that cans clog before they are empty, and that it failed as a permanent roof or plumbing repair. Most of these trace back to prep, coat thickness or using it beyond what the maker supports.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use Flex Seal on a basement wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On small spots, yes. Flex Seal Liquid can seal around pipe penetrations, window well seams and the outside of a foundation crack. As a whole-wall waterproofer it is a poor choice: it has no published pressure rating, water from the soil side tends to push it off, and a masonry waterproofer covers the same wall for far less money.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint over Flex Seal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the product. The maker says the spray can be painted any color once it is dry. For Flex Seal Liquid, the maker advises against painting over it. Flex Tape mostly will not take paint, though the maker suggests a coat of the spray as a primer if you want to try.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Flex Seal safe for drinking water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The maker says the spray is not for surfaces that contact drinking water, and Flex Tape has not been checked for drinking-water contact. Flex Seal Liquid's page says it is safe for rain barrels once fully cured but does not address potable water. Do not use any of them on water tanks, wells or supply pipes that carry drinking water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are UAL Flex sealant reviews about Flex Seal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not necessarily. We found no product sold under the exact name UAL Flex, and several unrelated sealants use Flex in their names, including automotive seam sealers and construction silicones. Check the maker on the label before reading reviews. Flex Seal itself is sold by the Flex Seal Family of Products through flexsealproducts.com and major retailers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does Flex Seal take to dry?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Per the maker, the spray is dry to the touch in about 2 to 3 hours and fully cured in 24 to 48 hours. Flex Seal Liquid is dry to the touch in 6 to 12 hours and cures in 24 to 48 hours. Wait 24 to 48 hours between coats for both, and longer in cool or humid weather.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
